.TH "GIT\-RECOMMIT" "1" "" "git\-recommit" "Git Recommit Manual"
.SH NAME
git\-recommit \- rebuild a run of commits into reviewable ones, in a TUI
.SH SYNOPSIS
\f[B]git recommit\f[R] [\f[B]\-C\f[R] \f[I]directory\f[R]]
[\f[B]\-\-root\f[R]] [\f[B]\-\-validate\f[R] \f[I]command\f[R]]
[\f[B]\-u\f[R]] [\f[B]\-\-edit\f[R] \f[I]commit\f[R]] [\f[I]range\f[R]]
.SH DESCRIPTION
Rebuild a run of commits into new ones that make sense to review,
choosing in a TUI which changes go in each.
.PP
You made a feature in a dozen commits, and they tell the story of how
you got there: the first version is very different from the last.
\f[CR]git recommit\f[R] shows you where you ended up, as one diff, and
you build new commits from it, a line, a hunk or a file at a time.
If you ask it to, it runs your tests on each new commit before replacing
the old ones.
.SH USAGE
.IP
.EX
git recommit HEAD\(ti6                       \f[I]# rebuild the last 6 commits\f[R]
git recommit HEAD\(ti6 \-\-validate \(aqpytest\(aq   \f[I]# ...and run pytest on each new commit\f[R]
git recommit main..HEAD                   \f[I]# or a range, as git reads it (.. or ...)\f[R]
git recommit HEAD                         \f[I]# split up only your uncommitted changes\f[R]
git recommit \-u HEAD\(ti2                    \f[I]# with untracked files too\f[R]
git recommit \-\-root                       \f[I]# every commit, as git rebase \-\-root\f[R]
git recommit \-\-edit HEAD\(ti3                \f[I]# change one commit, then replay the later ones on it\f[R]
.EE
.PP
The range is read as git reads ranges: \f[CR]a..b\f[R] is the commits in
\f[CR]b\f[R] but not \f[CR]a\f[R] (so \f[CR]main..HEAD\f[R] is those
since your branch left \f[CR]main\f[R]), \f[CR]a...b\f[R] those on
either side but not both, and \f[CR]a\f[R] alone means
\f[CR]a..HEAD\f[R].
It can end below \f[CR]HEAD\f[R] (e.g.
\f[CR]HEAD\(ti6..HEAD\(ti2\f[R]): then the commits after it are replayed
on the new ones, as \f[CR]git rebase\f[R] does, which needs the last new
commit to end as the range does (every change assigned).
It can\(aqt have merge commits in it, or after it.
Your uncommitted changes to tracked files (staged or not) are included,
after the last commit, so you can assign them to new commits too.
Untracked files are too with \f[CR]\-u\f[R]
(\f[CR]\-\-include\-untracked\f[R]), as with \f[CR]git stash \-u\f[R],
but for ignored ones; without it, the sidebar says how many are left
out.
Untracked files you don\(aqt assign are left untracked.
.SS The TUI
The sidebar on the left lists the new commits you\(aqre building; the
one marked ▶ is the \f[I]active\f[R] commit; click one to make it
active.
Below them is the range being replaced (check it\(aqs the one you
meant), then the files, each with how many of its changes are assigned;
click one to go to it.
.PP
The new commits start out as the commits being rebuilt, with their
messages and authors, each taking the changes it made.
Where an old commit\(aqs changes overlap a later one\(aqs (e.g.
it added a line a later commit rewrote), it gets an edit (✎) so that
it\(aqs as it was.
Writing them unchanged makes the same commits again.
Change what you like: drop an edit (\f[CR]E\f[R]), move changes, add or
delete commits.
\f[CR]C\f[R] clears them all, to start from scratch, and \f[CR]O\f[R]
brings them back as they were.
The sidebar scrolls when it\(aqs too long.
The main panel is the diff from the start of the range to the end, with
each line\(aqs numbers before and after, and a rule across the panel
where each hunk starts.
Each change is marked, at the far left, with the number of the commit
that takes it (\f[CR]·\f[R] for none yet).
The keys are along the top.
.PP
