.PP
Each new commit has every change assigned to it or to an earlier commit.
.PP
\f[B]A renamed file\f[R] is one file, shown as \f[CR]R old \-> new\f[R],
as \f[CR]git status\f[R] shows it, found as \f[CR]git diff\f[R] finds
renames (unless \f[CR]diff.renames\f[R] is off).
The rename is a change of its own, on a row under the file\(aqs name,
apart from the changes to its lines (but together with a change of its
mode, if it has one): until a commit takes the rename, the file is at
its old path, with whatever lines it takes.
\f[CR]f\f[R] assigns all of it.
.PP
\f[B]Editing a file in a commit\f[R] (\f[CR]e\f[R]) is for making a
commit work on its own, e.g.
stubbing out a function that a later commit fills in.
The edit is carried into the later commits, merged with the changes they
take, as a rebase would.
Where a later commit\(aqs changes overlap the edit, that commit
conflicts: it\(aqs marked in the sidebar, and you resolve it by editing
the file in that commit, which opens with conflict markers (but for a
binary file or symlink, or a file git takes for binary, below).
You can\(aqt write until every conflict is resolved.
.PP
\f[CR]e\f[R] opens the file as it is in the active commit; if the commit
doesn\(aqt have the file yet, it opens the file\(aqs end state, for you
to cut down.
To cancel, quit without saving (or make the editor fail, e.g.
\f[CR]:cq\f[R] in vim): saving what was opened changes nothing.
.PP
\f[CR]+\f[R] does the same for a file the range doesn\(aqt change (or
that isn\(aqt there at all), e.g.
to stub one out for a commit and put it back in a later one; it\(aqs
shown as \f[CR]= path\f[R].
.PP
A binary file doesn\(aqt open in an editor: \f[CR]e\f[R] asks for a file
to replace it with in the commit (relative to the current directory).
A symlink opens as where it points.
Since neither can be merged, a later commit that changes the file too
conflicts with the edit, until you replace (or edit) it there as well.
There, it opens as your edit made it (there\(aqs nothing to mark), and
saving it unchanged (or giving the same file) keeps that, resolving the
conflict; so to cancel, make the editor fail (\f[CR]:cq\f[R]).
Submodules can\(aqt be edited.
.PP
A file with a NUL in its first 8000 bytes is one git takes for binary.
If its attributes have it diffed as text (e.g.
\f[CR]diff\f[R]), it opens in the editor, but git won\(aqt merge it, so
it conflicts as a binary file does, and opens the same way.
.PP
Where the edit carried in deleted such a file (or a binary file or
symlink), the commit that conflicts doesn\(aqt have it, so \f[CR]e\f[R]
opens its end state (or, for a binary file, asks for a file).
Saving that unchanged (or giving that same file) keeps the deletion,
resolving the conflict, as does
\f[CR]git recommit edit N path \-\-delete\f[R].
To have the file there as it ends up instead, edit it to anything, then
back.
.PP
\f[B]Changing your mind\f[R] about the code itself: press
\f[CR]ctrl+z\f[R], change files (or even commit), then \f[CR]fg\f[R].
git\-recommit reloads the diff, and changes you\(aqd assigned keep their
commits; new ones are unassigned.
.PP
\f[B]Writing\f[R] (\f[CR]w\f[R]) asks first, and warns about anything
that isn\(aqt assigned to a commit.
Changes you leave unassigned aren\(aqt lost: they\(aqre left as
uncommitted changes in your working tree, which is never touched (though
nothing is left staged, but for new files, which are left
\f[CR]git add \-\-intent\-to\-add\f[R]ed, so they\(aqre still new files
rather than untracked ones).
With \f[CR]\-\-validate\f[R], the command runs in a scratch worktree
with each new commit checked out in turn (with your
\f[CR]post\-checkout\f[R] hook, as any checkout runs it, e.g.
for Git LFS); if it fails, you choose to go back and \f[B]e\f[R]dit the
commits, \f[B]a\f[R]bort, or \f[B]p\f[R]roceed anyway.
.PP
A commit that starts from an old one keeps its author; one you create is
yours, as with \f[CR]git commit\f[R].
Writing warns about any commit whose author isn\(aqt who made some of
its changes, and \f[CR]A\f[R] (or \f[CR]git recommit commit author\f[R])
sets a commit\(aqs author, as \f[CR]git commit \-\-author\f[R] does.
.PP
New commits are signed if \f[CR]commit.gpgSign\f[R] says to, and the
\f[CR]commit\-msg\f[R] hook runs on the messages of commits you create
or reword (not on old commits\(aq messages you kept), as with
\f[CR]git rebase\f[R]; no other hooks run.
.PP
The old commits are replaced, and the command prints how to get them
back (\f[CR]git reset <old HEAD>\f[R], which leaves your working tree as
it is); they\(aqre also in the reflog.
.PP
Only the branch at \f[CR]HEAD\f[R] moves.
Another branch built on the old commits still has them: rebase it onto
the new ones with
\f[CR]git rebase \-\-onto <new tip> <old tip> <branch>\f[R], which
replays only its own commits, or, if a stacking tool (e.g.
\c
.UR https://github.com/benekastah/git-stack
git\-stack
.UE \c
) tracks your branches, with that tool, since it records what each
branch was built on.
.SS From the command line
Everything the TUI does can be done a step at a time from the command
line, e.g.
in a script, or by an agent.
\f[CR]start\f[R] begins a recommit, kept as a plan in the git directory
(like a rebase in progress) until \f[CR]write\f[R] makes the new
commits, or \f[CR]abort\f[R] drops it.
Commits are named by number, from 1, and changes by \f[I]targets\f[R]:
\f[CR]PATH\f[R] (the whole file), \f[CR]PATH:\(atN\f[R] (its Nth hunk;
\f[CR]\(at0\f[R] is the change to the file itself, like its mode or
renaming it), \f[CR]PATH:+N\-M\f[R] (added lines, numbered as after the
changes) or \f[CR]PATH:\-N\-M\f[R] (removed lines, numbered as before).
\f[CR]show\f[R] shows the numbers.
\f[CR]PATH\f[R] is a git pathspec, as in \f[CR]git add\f[R]: relative to
the current directory, a directory for the files in it, wildcards like
\f[CR]\(aqsrc/*.py\(aq\f[R], and magic like \f[CR]:/\f[R] (from the top)
or \f[CR]:(literal)\f[R]; a hunk or lines need it to match one file.
A renamed file goes by either of its paths.
.IP
.EX
git recommit start HEAD\(ti6               \f[I]# start from the 6 commits as they are\f[R]
git recommit show                       \f[I]# the commits, and the diff marked with them\f[R]
git recommit show \-\-json                \f[I]# all of it, for scripts\f[R]
git recommit commit add \(dqAdd the API\(dq \-\-after 2
git recommit assign 3 src/api.py src/util.py:\(at2 src/main.py:+10\-14
git recommit unassign src/main.py:\-3
git recommit edit 3 src/api.py < stub.py    \f[I]# the file in commit 3, by hand\f[R]
git recommit edit 3 logo.png < old\-logo.png \f[I]# (a binary file too)\f[R]
git recommit edit 2 scratch.txt \-\-delete    \f[I]# (any file, changed by the range or not)\f[R]
git recommit commit move 3 1                \f[I]# or remove, or reword\f[R]
git recommit commit author 3 \(dqAna <ana\(atexample.com>\(dq
git recommit write \-\-validate \(aqpytest\(aq
git recommit tui                        \f[I]# or carry on in the TUI (quitting keeps the plan)\f[R]
git recommit abort                      \f[I]# or drop it\f[R]
.EE
.PP
The other subcommands are \f[CR]drop\-edit\f[R], \f[CR]clear\f[R],
\f[CR]restore\f[R] and \f[CR]install\-skills\f[R] (see below);
\f[CR]git recommit <subcommand> \-h\f[R] says more.
With \f[CR]\-\-validate\f[R], \f[CR]write\f[R] stops at the first commit
the command fails on, changing nothing.
.SS \-\-edit
\f[CR]git recommit \-\-edit <commit>\f[R] runs \f[CR]git rebase \-i\f[R]
and stops at that commit.
Change it and \f[CR]git commit \-\-amend\f[R], then
\f[CR]git rebase \-\-continue\f[R] replays the later commits, stopping
for you to resolve any conflicts.
.SS Agent skills
A skill for coding agents,
\f[CR]git_recommit/skills/git\-recommit/SKILL.md\f[R], tells an agent
how to rebuild commits with the command line above, never the TUI.
It asks not to be used unless you ask, which Claude Code honours and
other agents may not.
It ships in the package, and
.IP
.EX
git recommit install\-skills
.EE
.PP
links it into the skills directory of each agent it finds you use
(\f[CR]\-\-claude\f[R] and \f[CR]\-\-codex\f[R] name one, even if it
isn\(aqt found; \f[CR]\-\-json\f[R] says what it did): Claude\(aqs is
\f[CR]\(ti/.claude/skills\f[R], or \f[CR]$CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR/skills\f[R];
Codex\(aqs is \f[CR]\(ti/.agents/skills\f[R].
The links point into the installed package, so upgrading git\-recommit
updates them.
.IP \(bu 2
A link already right is left alone, and so is one of two agents\(aq that
share a skills directory.
.IP \(bu 2
A link to another directory (another clone\(aqs, or a skill of your own)
is left too, with nothing linked anywhere, and it stops saying so;
\f[CR]\-\-replace\f[R] takes it over.
A link whose target is gone is replaced.
A real directory or file is never touched: move it away.
.IP \(bu 2
If it finds no agent and none is named, it says so and does nothing,
which isn\(aqt a failure.
.IP \(bu 2
On Windows, where a symlink needs a privilege, it makes a junction.
